Assume real numbers  $a_i,b_i\,(i=0,1,\cdots,2n)$  satisfy the following conditions:
(1) for  $i=0,1,\cdots,2n-1$ , we have  $a_i+a_{i+1}\geq 0$ ;
(2) for  $j=0,1,\cdots,n-1$ , we have  $a_{2j+1}\leq 0$ ;
(2) for any integer  $p,q$ ,  $0\leq p\leq q\leq n$ , we have  $\sum_{k=2p}^{2q}b_k>0$ .
Prove that  $\sum_{i=0}^{2n}(-1)^i a_i b_i\geq 0$ , and determine when the equality holds.

Given \( a_{1}=1 \) and \( a_{n}=n^{2} \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{k^{2}} (n \geqslant 2) \), prove that:
1. \( \frac{a_{n}+1}{a_{n+1}} = \frac{n^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}} (n \geqslant 2) \);
2. \( \left(1+\frac{1}{a_{1}}\right)\left(1+\frac{1}{a_{2}}\right) \cdots\left(1+\frac{1}{a_{n}}\right) < 4 (n \geqslant 1) \).

In an $n \times 2n$ grid (where $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $n > 1$), each cell is filled with either a "+" sign or a "-" sign. A sequence of integers $\left(x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{2n}\right)$ is considered "optimal" if it satisfies the following properties:
(i) $\sum_{i=1}^{2n} |x_{i}| \neq 0$;
(ii) $|x_{i}| \leq n$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, 2n$;
(iii) Each column $i$ of the $n \times 2n$ grid has its cell filled with a "+" or "-" sign followed by $x_{i}$ for $i = 1, 2, \ldots, 2n$ such that the sum of the numbers in each row is zero for rows $j = 1, 2, \ldots, n$.

Prove that there exist at least four different "optimal" sequences.

Consider an arbitrary triangle $A B C$. Let $A_{1}, B_{1}, C_{1}$ be three points on the lines $B C$, $C A$, and $A B$, respectively. Define the following:

$$
\begin{aligned}
R & =\frac{\left|A C_{1}\right|}{\left|C_{1} B\right|} \cdot \frac{\left|B A_{1}\right|}{\left|A_{1} C\right|} \cdot \frac{\left|C B_{1}\right|}{\left|B_{1} A\right|} \\
R^{*} & =\frac{\sin \widehat{A C C_{1}}}{\sin \widehat{C_{1} C B}} \cdot \frac{\sin \widehat{B A A_{1}}}{\sin \widehat{A_{1} A C}} \cdot \frac{\sin \widehat{C B B_{1}}}{\sin \widehat{B_{1} B A}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Prove that $R=R^{*}$.

Let \(ABCD\) be a rectangle with an area of 2, \(P\) be a point on side \(CD\), and \(Q\) be the point where the incircle of \(\triangle PAB\) is tangent to side \(AB\). The value of the product \(PA \cdot PB\) varies as point \(P\) changes. When \(PA \cdot PB\) reaches its minimum value,
